(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["intro-to-pa-profession-pa-intro-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Intro to PA Profession Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Intro to PA","questions":[{"q":"New health professions have historically arisen as generalist physician substitutes, a role termed:","choices":["Physician dependent","Physician subordinate","Physician complementary","Physician equivalent"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture describes newly emerging health professions as filling gaps left by physician shortages using the terms \"physician-substitute\" and \"physician-complementary,\" with physician complementary capturing the role's function of extending physician care rather than replacing it entirely. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"Thelma Ingles, an RN at Duke, attempted to develop an early midlevel provider but could not obtain accreditation from the:","choices":["National League of Nursing","The AMA House of Delegates","The newly formed ARC-PA commission","The NBME certifying board"],"answer":0,"exp":"Per the lecture, Thelma Ingles, an RN at Duke, developed an early midlevel-provider curriculum but was denied accreditation by the National League of Nursing, illustrating nursing's early resistance to an expanded RN role. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"At Emory in the 1940s, Stead trained physicians using what type of curriculum?","choices":["Research-based","Competency-based","Lecture-based","Apprenticeship-only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Dr. Eugene Stead used a fast-track, competency-based training model at Emory in the 1940s to staff Grady Hospital, an approach he later carried into the Duke PA program design. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"The Feldsher role, still present today, originated in which century in Russia?","choices":["1900s","1600s","1400s","1800s"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture's table of predecessor non-physician providers lists Russian Feldshers as originating in the 1600s and persisting to the present day. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"Which stage focuses on 'public policy analysis of the success and acceptance of the new profession'?","choices":["Incorporation","Implementation","Evaluation","Ideology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stage III, Evaluation, is defined in the lecture as the public-policy analysis of the new profession's cost-effectiveness, acceptance by professionals and patients, and quality of care. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"The GMENAC report is associated with which stage of PA development?","choices":["Incorporation","Maturation","Ideology","Implementation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The GMENAC report is tied to Stage IV, Incorporation, the phase covering expanded utilization, new regulations, and secured reimbursement (including Medicare) as the profession grew. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"Which magazine/TV portrayal in the 1990s helped the public view the PA profession positively?","choices":["Scrubs","House","ER","Grey's Anatomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture credits the 1990s TV show ER with helping present a positive public image of the PA profession during that decade's growth. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"The Balanced Budget Act that expanded CMS reimbursement for PAs was passed in:","choices":["1997","2001","1986","1993"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Balanced Budget Act of 1997 expanded CMS/Medicare reimbursement for PA services to all settings at 85% of the physician fee schedule. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"In 2001, which centralized application service was created?","choices":["PACKRAT","CAQ","OSCE","CASPA"],"answer":3,"exp":"CASPA, the Centralized Application Service for PAs, was created in 2001, the same year ARC-PA became a free-standing accrediting body and the PA History Society was founded. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"Pi Alpha Honor Society was organized in what year?","choices":["2010","2001","2012","2004"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pi Alpha Honor Society was organized in 2004, per the lecture's decade-by-decade timeline of the 2000s. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"Which was rated #1 by Forbes and Money magazine (2012)?","choices":["The pharmacy degree","The MD degree","The PA degree","The nursing degree"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture notes that in 2012, the PA degree was rated #1 by Forbes and Money magazine, coinciding with the profession certifying its 100,000th PA. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"As of the lecture, the Licensure Compact had been adopted by approximately how many states?","choices":["8","24","16","50"],"answer":2,"exp":"As of the lecture, the Licensure Compact — which lets a PA licensed in one member state gain practice authorization in other member states more easily — had been adopted by 16 states, with 12 more having filed. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"NCCPA launched PANRE-LA after a 2-year pilot study in which year?","choices":["2025","2021","2017","2023"],"answer":3,"exp":"NCCPA launched PANRE-LA, the longitudinal recertification assessment (25 questions quarterly across 12 quarters), in 2023 after a two-year pilot study. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"The PA History Society was founded in what year?","choices":["1997","2001","2010","1974"],"answer":1,"exp":"The PA History Society (PAHx) was founded in 2001, the same year CASPA launched and ARC-PA became a free-standing accrediting body. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"Which value of PAHx involves involving the public, PAs, and PA programs in collecting history?","choices":["Recognition","Credibility","Engagement","Dedication"],"answer":2,"exp":"PAHx's stated values include Recognition & Understanding, Dedication, Credibility, and Engagement — Engagement specifically reflects involving the public, PAs, and PA programs in collecting and sharing the profession's history. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — History of the PA Profession"},{"q":"ARC-PA is composed of up to how many Commissioners?","choices":["30","20","7","50"],"answer":0,"exp":"ARC-PA is composed of up to 30 Commissioners, with two-thirds being PAs and the remainder made up of up to 6 physicians, 2 deans, and 2 public members. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"Post-professional PA doctoral program accreditation standards became effective when?","choices":["9/2020","9/2025","9/2019","9/2026"],"answer":1,"exp":"ARC-PA's Post-Professional PA Doctoral Program (PPDP) accreditation standards became effective in September 2025. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"Which organization administers End-of-Curriculum exams?","choices":["ARC-PA","PAEA","AAPA","NCCPA"],"answer":1,"exp":"PAEA administers assessment tools including PACKRAT as well as End-of-Rotation and End-of-Curriculum exams for PA students. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"The NCCPA Board includes representatives from AAPA, PAEA, and:","choices":["ARC-PA","WHO","CMS","FSMB"],"answer":3,"exp":"The NCCPA Board includes representatives from AAPA, PAEA, and FSMB, alongside 7 PA directors-at-large, 2 public members, and 5 physician directors. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"Which of the following is a benefit of NCCPA certification?","choices":["Signals commitment to lifelong learning","Guaranteed lifetime job placement","A full lifetime exemption from CME","An automatic state medical license"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture lists signaling a commitment to lifelong learning as a key benefit of NCCPA certification, alongside easing moves between specialties/states and satisfying many states' licensure/prescribing requirements. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"NCCPA Code of Conduct actions may be reported to the FSMB, state licensing authorities, the federal government, and the PA's:","choices":["Family","Patients","School","Employer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Per the lecture, NCCPA Code of Conduct violations may be reported to the FSMB, state licensing authorities, the federal government, and the PA's employer. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"AAPA values include Leadership and Service, Unity and Transparency, Accountability, and:","choices":["Advocacy efforts","Excellence and Equity","Certification status","Program accreditation"],"answer":1,"exp":"AAPA's stated values include Leadership and Service, Unity and Transparency, Accountability, and Excellence and Equity. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"The AAPA maintains which annual publication about compensation?","choices":["Annual Salary Report","JAMA","NCCPA Statistical Profile","JAAPA Salary Digest"],"answer":0,"exp":"AAPA publishes an Annual Salary Report as one of its member benefits and advocacy tools regarding PA compensation. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"Which is an example of an AAPA caucus?","choices":["Judicial Affairs","Board of Directors","Veterans Caucus","House of Delegates"],"answer":2,"exp":"AAPA has 15 caucuses and special interest groups, of which a Veterans Caucus is a representative example. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"The SAAAPA Assembly of Representatives serves as the:","choices":["Policy-making body of the Student Academy","The accrediting body for PA programs","The certifying body for individual PAs","A state-level licensing authority"],"answer":0,"exp":"The SAAAPA Assembly of Representatives (AOR) functions as the policy-making body of the Student Academy, mirroring how the AAPA House of Delegates functions for the full membership. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"FAPA fellow (full) membership costs approximately how much per year?","choices":["$195","$75","$60","$305"],"answer":0,"exp":"According to the lecture, FAPA — Florida's only professional PA organization, with membership tiered by career stage — sets full/fellow membership at approximately $195 per year, distinct from its discounted $60 flat-rate student membership. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"The Florida PA Council is composed of 3 PAs, 1 allopathic physician, and:","choices":["1 licensed pharmacist","2 appointed public members","1 registered nurse","1 osteopathic physician"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Florida PA Council is composed of 3 PAs, 1 allopathic physician, and 1 osteopathic physician, per the lecture's licensure/credentialing section — each physician member must also supervise a PA. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"PAF programs include a podcast called:","choices":["Stead Stories","Huddle","Vital Minds","PA Talk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Per the lecture, one of PAF's outreach programs is a podcast called Vital Minds, part of its broader programming on pain management, telemedicine, mental health, and substance-use-disorder prevention. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"The AAPA benefit 'Maxwell Quick Medical Reference' is available to which members?","choices":["Retired members","Students","Physicians","Fellows only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Maxwell Quick Medical Reference is listed among AAPA's student membership benefits, alongside JAAPA, PA School Survival Guides, and PAF scholarship eligibility. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"Which PA organization administers assessment tools like PACKRAT?","choices":["ARC-PA","NCCPA","PAEA","AAPA"],"answer":2,"exp":"PAEA administers PACKRAT along with End-of-Rotation and End-of-Curriculum exams as part of its mission to advance PA education. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PA Organizations"},{"q":"Which PA competency domain includes 'improve the health of patient populations'?","choices":["Practice-based learning","Society and population health","Person-centered care domain","Knowledge for practice"],"answer":1,"exp":"Society and Population Health is the competency domain focused on understanding determinants of health and improving the health of patient populations, one of the seven domains in the \"Knowledgeable Interpersonal People Interact Professionally, Practicing for Society\" mnemonic. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Recently adopted competencies specifically address the:","choices":["PA educator","Physician supervisor","Retiring PA","New PA graduate"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture notes that recently adopted Core Competencies specifically address the New PA Graduate, reflecting the 2021 revision's focus on entry-level practice readiness. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Which is required to sit for the PANCE?","choices":["Graduation from an ARC-PA accredited program","Five years of clinical experience","Obtaining state licensure first","Completion of a doctoral degree"],"answer":0,"exp":"Graduation from an ARC-PA accredited program is required to sit for the PANCE, which candidates take shortly after their expected graduation date through Pearson VUE. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Florida licensure requires submission of how many CME hours every 2 years (or evidence of NCCPA cert)?","choices":["50","75","100","150"],"answer":2,"exp":"Florida licensure requires submission of 100 CME hours every 2 years, or evidence of current NCCPA certification in lieu of the CME requirement. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"The Florida PA license application fee is:","choices":["$100","$500","$305","$195"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Florida PA license application fee is $305 (which includes a $100 non-refundable portion), per the lecture's licensure section. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Florida PA applicants must use an approved service for criminal background screening called:","choices":["E-Verify","CASPA","NPDB","Livescan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Florida PA license applicants must use Livescan, the state's approved electronic fingerprinting/criminal background screening service, as part of the application process. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Which statement about licensure is TRUE?","choices":["It is entirely optional in most states","It automatically creates autonomous practice","It does not necessarily imply autonomous practice","It fully replaces national certification"],"answer":2,"exp":"Licensure is the most rigorous method of professional regulation and no PA may practice without one, but the lecture is explicit that licensure does not by itself create autonomous practice. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Telehealth requires that a PA be licensed in which state?","choices":["The state where the patient is located","Any state the PA chooses to select","No license is required at all","Only the PA's own home state"],"answer":0,"exp":"For telehealth, a PA must be licensed in the state where the patient is physically located at the time of the encounter, not merely where the PA themselves resides or practices. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Which is required for informed consent for a procedure under anesthesia, per CMS?","choices":["The person performing the procedure obtains it","The patient signs without explanation","Only a licensed nurse may obtain it","Any available staff member may obtain it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Per CMS requirements described in the lecture, informed consent for a procedure performed under anesthesia must be obtained by the person actually performing the procedure. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"An occurrence malpractice policy covers incidents:","choices":["Only after separate tail coverage is purchased","While the policy was in effect regardless of claim date","Never for any practicing PA, under any circumstance","Only while the policy remains actively in force"],"answer":1,"exp":"An occurrence policy covers incidents that happened while the policy was in effect, regardless of when the claim is later filed, so no additional \"tail\" coverage is needed after the policy ends. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Which federal program is the largest employee health program in the US?","choices":["Medicare, covering roughly 70 million beneficiaries","TRICARE, covering the uniformed services","Medicaid, covering roughly 74.8 million beneficiaries","Federal Employee Health Benefits Program"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Federal Employee Health Benefits Program (FEHB), covering 8M+ people, is described in the lecture as the largest employee health program in the US. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Medicare Part D covers:","choices":["Outpatient services","Prescription drugs","Managed care","Hospital services"],"answer":1,"exp":"Medicare Part D covers prescription drugs, distinct from Part A (hospital), Part B (outpatient), and Part C (Medicare Advantage/managed care). — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"PAs may own what percentage of a corporation if state law allows?","choices":["51%","100%","49%","85%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Per the reimbursement section of the lecture, PAs can own 100% of a corporation if state law allows, reflecting expanding practice-ownership opportunities. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"The transformation in US healthcare reimbursement is from fee-for-service toward:","choices":["Value-based care","Cash-only","Bundled surgery only","Capitation only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lecture describes an ongoing transformation in US healthcare reimbursement moving from fee-for-service toward value-based care, including models like PCMHs and ACOs. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Privileging may require certifications such as:","choices":["The CAQ","ACLS/PALS","The PANCE","A CASPA form"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lecture lists procedure logs, specialty CME, simulation lab work, ACLS/PALS certification, and peer attestation as examples of what privileging (granted at the institutional level and renewed annually) may require. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Which is the LEAST rigorous credential compared to licensure?","choices":["A doctoral-level academic degree","A post-graduate fellowship credential","Exam-based board certification","Registration or certification"],"answer":3,"exp":"Registration or certification is described as a less rigorous form of professional regulation than licensure, which the lecture calls the most rigorous method. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Interprofessional education (IPE) primarily involves professionals learning:","choices":["Entirely in isolation, alone","Only in clinical settings","Only from physicians alone","With, from, and about each other"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture's WHO-based definition of interprofessional education/collaboration describes multiple health professionals learning and working with, from, and about each other to deliver the highest-quality patient care. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"},{"q":"A Clinical Nurse Specialist requires which degree beyond RN?","choices":["Associate's","Master's degree","Doctorate only","Certificate only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Per the lecture's provider-reference table, a Clinical Nurse Specialist is an RN who has additionally earned a master's degree and become licensed in a specialized area such as diabetes or critical care. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"},{"q":"Which provider works under the direction of a physical therapist and applies modalities?","choices":["Recreation therapist","Athletic trainer role","Occupational therapist","Physical therapy assistant"],"answer":3,"exp":"The physical therapy assistant works under the direction of a licensed physical therapist and applies treatment modalities as part of the rehabilitation team. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"},{"q":"A pharmacist's newest expanded role, varying by state, is:","choices":["Radiology","Surgical care","Anesthesia","Prescribing"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture identifies prescribing, which varies by state, as the pharmacist's newest expanded scope-of-practice role. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"},{"q":"Which provider takes x-rays and may specialize in CT and MRI?","choices":["Radiology technologist","Medical lab technologist","Nuclear medicine tech","Respiratory therapist"],"answer":0,"exp":"The radiology technologist, per the lecture's provider table, takes x-rays and may go on to specialize in advanced imaging modalities like CT and MRI. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"},{"q":"A nuclear medicine technician prepares and administers what to patients?","choices":["Anesthetic agents","Antibiotic medications","Radioactive drugs","Donated blood products"],"answer":2,"exp":"The nuclear medicine technician prepares and administers radioactive drugs to patients for diagnostic imaging or treatment purposes, per the lecture's provider table. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"},{"q":"Which is one of the four tenets of patient-centered care?","choices":["Collaboration","Consolidation","Competition","Certification"],"answer":0,"exp":"The four tenets of patient-centered care per the lecture are Dignity & Respect, Information Sharing, Participation, and Collaboration. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"},{"q":"A recreation therapist is also referred to as a(n):","choices":["Rehabilitation counselor","Occupational therapist role","Therapeutic recreation specialist","Licensed physical therapist"],"answer":2,"exp":"A recreation therapist is also referred to as a therapeutic recreation specialist, per the lecture's provider-reference table. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"},{"q":"Continuous care in primary care means care is provided:","choices":["In hospitals only","By specialists only","Throughout the years","Only during emergencies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Continuous, one of primary care's \"5 C's,\" means care is provided throughout the years, reflecting an ongoing relationship rather than isolated encounters. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PAs & Clinical Practice"},{"q":"Which describes coordinated care?","choices":["Emergency-only care","Care with specialists","Care in isolation","Care without referrals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Coordinated, another of primary care's \"5 C's,\" refers to care that is coordinated with specialists as needed, ensuring the primary provider manages referrals and communication across the care team. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — PAs & Clinical Practice"},{"q":"Failure of a strong team can lead to medical errors, impacting patient safety and potentially causing:","choices":["Accreditation","Better outcomes","Higher salaries","Malpractice"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture states that failure of a strong healthcare team can lead to medical errors, impacting patient safety and potentially resulting in malpractice claims. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professionalism"},{"q":"Which is TRUE about professionalism per the lecture?","choices":["It cannot be taught","It is unrelated to practice","It is defined by a single quality","It is a combination of qualities"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture defines professionalism as not one single quality but a combination of qualities — responsibility, ethics, accountability, integrity, honesty, and others — that takes time to develop. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professionalism"},{"q":"Which document must support medical necessity and the level of service billed?","choices":["The provider's CME log","The clinical documentation/note","The malpractice insurance policy","The state license application"],"answer":1,"exp":"The clinical documentation/note must support medical necessity and the level of service billed, serving legal, quality-of-care, and payment purposes as described in the lecture's documentation section. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Professional Practice"},{"q":"Which best captures the purpose of interprofessional collaboration?","choices":["Deliver the highest quality of care as a team","Increase clinical documentation volume","Reduce staffing costs alone, nothing else","Eliminate physician roles entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"The WHO-based definition in the lecture frames interprofessional collaboration's purpose as multiple professionals working with patients, families, and communities to deliver the highest quality of care as a team. — Intro to PA Profession lecture — Health Care is Teamwork"}]};
